Apple's new Texture and Grain controls for Photographic Styles are not available on iPhones other than the latest iPhone 18 Pro models and upcoming iPhone Duo, despite the company's original wording suggesting otherwise.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/customize-texture-photographic-styles-3.jpeg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1066603" /><br/>
When Apple announced the iPhone 18 Pro and iPhone 18 Pro Max earlier this month, its Newsroom press release said the new Texture and Grain controls would be "available in iOS 27 on iPhone models that support the latest generation of Photographic Styles."<br/>
<br/>
That original wording is preserved in copies of Apple's launch-day press release distributed through <a href="https://www.fidelity.com/news/article/default/202609091330BIZWIRE_USPR_____20260909_BW172657"><em>Business Wire</em></a> and <em><a href="https://noticenearby.com/press-releases/apple-apple-debuts-iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-18-pro-max-nre7aff9">Notice Nearby</a></em>. Elsewhere, in Apple's User Guide for iOS 27, devices that support the "latest generation Photographic Styles" are <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-gb/guide/iphone/iph629d2cd37/27/ios/27">listed</a> as the iPhone 16 series, iPhone 17 series, iPhone Air, and iPhone 18 Pro models. <br/>
<br/>
Apple has since <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/09/apple-debuts-iphone-18-pro-and-iphone-18-pro-max/">changed the footnote</a> on its iPhone 18 Pro Newsroom announcement. It now says Texture and Grain controls are available "only for photos captured on iPhone 18 Pro models and with the Ultra Wide, Main, and Center Stage cameras on the iPhone Duo."<br/>
<br/>
Apple also states definitively in its <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-gb/guide/iphone/iph629d2cd37/27/ios/27">iOS 27 User Guide</a> that changing the texture of a photo can be achieved "on iPhone 18 Pro models."<br/>
